-- Captured rows for the local stack's warehouse, applied by compose/seed-catalog.py.
--
-- ROWS ONLY. The CREATE TABLE for each of these is generated by the seed script
-- from redash.historical.schema's own build_create_table_sql and map_column_type,
-- driven by the `columns` list in catalog.json. Hand-writing the DDL here would
-- be a second description of a table shape the fork already defines, and the
-- first schema change would leave the two disagreeing with nothing to notice.
--
-- __TABLE_<key>__ and __QID_<key>__ are substituted with the resolved historical
-- table name and Redash query id. Both are only known once the queries exist, so
-- neither can be written down here.
--
-- Timestamps are relative to now(), so a stack seeded today and one seeded in six
-- months both show recent coverage. A fixed date would age into a warehouse whose
-- freshest row is months old, which reads as a broken capture rather than a seed.

-- ── Bikeshare stations ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Dock inventory. Two captures a day apart: the set barely moves, which is what
-- makes it the reference dataset rather than a time series.

-- ── Bikeshare availability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- The time series half. Four captures over three days, with ST-0004 dropping to
-- zero bikes so an empty-state and an alert threshold both have something real
-- to fire on.

-- ── Transit vehicle positions ───────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Three lines, matching the route_labels on the transit data source, so a
-- colour-by-line visualization has more than one series.

-- ── Weather history ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- One row per capture, four days of them, so the coverage range on the dataset
-- page spans days rather than collapsing to a point.
